course: dictionary
lesson: 3149
title: The embassy: a country's office abroad
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An embassy is a special building in a foreign country. An ambassador and their staff work there to represent their own country, so they can speak for their home nation. The context shows this: «Lord Macartney came from Britain asking for more trade and an embassy in Beijing» means Britain wanted its own official building in Beijing.
explain: The word can also mean the group of people who work in that building. Check your meaning by swapping it into a sentence. «The British Embassy» still makes sense if you think of the office, and the people inside it.
explain: Countries keep embassies in each other's capital cities so they can talk and trade. The sentence «most countries keep their embassies in Tel Aviv» shows that embassies are not always in the same city as the government. Read the clues carefully and do not assume.
fact: An embassy is an official building in a foreign country.
fact: An ambassador works at an embassy to represent their own country.
fact: Embassy can also mean the group of people who work in that building.
fact: «Most countries keep their embassies in Tel Aviv» shows that embassies are placed in chosen cities, which may not be the same as the government's city.
fact: People often go to an embassy for help when they are abroad, such as getting a visa.
exercise: In «an embassy in Beijing», what is an embassy? | *A special building in a foreign country where a country's representatives work | A shop that sells British goods | A school for children from other countries | A park in the middle of a city
exercise: «Lord Macartney came from Britain asking for an embassy in Beijing.» What did Britain want? | A school for Chinese children | *A building in Beijing where its own representatives could work | A holiday in the emperor's palace | A new ship to sail home
exercise: Why do countries keep embassies in each other's capital cities? | So they can hide from each other | So they can play sports against each other | *So they can talk, trade and represent their own people | So they can build bigger castles
test: Besides the building, what else can embassy mean? | A kind of boat | A river in France | *The group of people who work there | A type of bread
test: Which phrase names an embassy of a country? | the British Castle | the British Garden | the British Harbour | *the British Embassy
exam: Using the sentences, why might a country want an embassy in another country's capital? | To grow more food | *To talk, trade and represent its own people there | To hide its boats | To build a bigger castle
